  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1... \nEastern CONUS surface high pressure remains the dominant weather\nfeature as it promotes dry weather and above normal temperature\nthis weekend into early next week. For those Trick- or-Treating\ntonight, this means temperatures hovering the 50s to lower 60s\nwith a slower nighttime cool off thanks to cirrus clouds aloft.\nWarm advection to start the week also means highs become 10-15 \ndegrees above normal by Wednesday (though no high temperature \nrecords are at risk). \n\nThe exception to the dry/warm forecast comes Monday as a\ndecaying trough lifts northward amid deeper trough development\nacross the Rockies, pushing a marginal warm front northward.\n6/12hr probabilities of measurable rainfall remain less than 10%\nbut 24hr probabilities approach 20% for eastern OH during this\ntime; this insinuates a non-zero chance for light rain/sprinkles\nthat is not currently forecasted because temporal variation \n(along with yes/no on occurrence) remains high. \n\nOutside of that, ensembles favor Wednesday night into the end \nof the week for an upper trough/surface low system to return \nrain to the region while dropping temperature closer toward\naverage (but remaining above normal). Variability remains in its\nstructure but the resultant impacts in a vast majority of those\nscenarios is very low.",
